{
  "answer_predetermined": false,
  "worthwhile": true,
  "derivation": "No algebraic identity or theorem fixes the outcome. The intervention adds loss term L_reg = 0.01 * 0.5 * (||W||_F - 0.85)^2 to cross-entropy. This modifies gradients: ∇W(L_total) = ∇W(L_CE) + 0.01 * (||W||_F - 0.85) * W/||W||_F. Whether this gradient perturbation improves held-out accuracy depends on: (1) whether the optimal ||W||_F for generalization on UCI Letter Recognition is near 0.85, (2) whether the 0.01 coefficient produces appropriate regularization strength, (3) whether SGD convergence with this penalty reaches a better local minimum. None of these are determined by the protocol specification. The Weibull growth law is explicitly noted as 'descriptive only' if refuted, confirming no theoretical guarantee exists. Control arm is valid (zero penalty, identical initialization/data/optimizer), so any difference must come from the regularization effect itself, which is empirically contingent.",
  "reason": "Genuine empirical uncertainty remains. Regularization effects on generalization are context-dependent (dataset structure, model capacity, hyperparameters). No theorem guarantees Frobenius norm constraint at scale 0.85 with coefficient 0.01 improves accuracy on this specific task. The hypothesis could be supported, refuted, or inconclusive based on actual training dynamics and test performance. This tests a novel operationalization of a descriptive law as prescriptive regularization, not a restatement of established results."
}

